Here is a robust, object-oriented solution in JavaScript. This approach uses a **Class-based design** to maintain the state of the queue internally, ensuring that the list is never lost between calls and that the order is strictly preserved.

### The Solution: `TicketQueue` Class

This implementation includes:
1.  **`addClient(name, ticketNumber)`**: Adds a new client to the end of the line.
2.  **`getNextClient()`**: Retrieves and removes the next person in line.
3.  **`getCurrentLine()`**: Returns a copy of the current queue (useful for displaying the waiting list).
4.  **`getQueueLength()`**: Helper to know how many people are waiting.

```javascript
class TicketQueue {
  constructor() {
    // Internal storage for the queue
    this.queue = [];
    this.lastTicket = 0; // Tracks the highest ticket number issued
  }

  /**
   * Adds a new client to the end of the line.
   * Automatically generates a ticket number if one isn't provided (or uses sequential logic).
   * @param {string} name - The client's name.
   * @param {number} [ticketNumber] - Optional specific ticket number. If omitted, generates next sequential.
   * @returns {object} The full details of the added client.
   */
  addClient(name, ticketNumber = null) {
    const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();
    const id = ticketNumber || this._generateTicketNumber();

    const newClient = {
      name,
      ticketNumber: id,
      enteredAt: timestamp
    };

    this.queue.push(newClient);
    console.log(`[System] Client "${name}" (Ticket #${id}) added to the line.`);

    return newClient;
  }

  /**
   * Retrieves and removes the next person in line.
   * Returns null if the queue is empty.
   * @returns {object|null} The next client object or null.
   */
  getNextClient() {
    if (this.queue.length === 0) {
      console.log("[System] Queue is empty. No one to call.");
      return null;
    }

    const nextClient = this.queue.shift(); // Removes the first element
    console.log(`[System] Call Ticket #${nextClient.ticketNumber}: ${nextClient.name}`);

    return nextClient;
  }

  /**
   * Returns a read-only copy of the current waiting line.
   * @returns {array} Array of client objects.
   */
  getCurrentLine() {
    return [...this.queue];
  }

  /**
   * Returns the total number of people currently waiting.
   * @returns {number}
   */
  getQueueLength() {
    return this.queue.length;
  }

  /**
   * Helper to generate the next ticket number (e.g., 101, 102, 103).
   * @private
   */
  _generateTicketNumber() {
    this.lastTicket++;
    return this.lastTicket;
  }
}

// ==========================================
// USAGE EXAMPLES
// ==========================================

// 1. Initialize the queue
const officeQueue = new TicketQueue();

// 2. Simulate clients arriving
console.log("--- Clients Arriving ---");
officeQueue.addClient("Alice");
officeQueue.addClient("Bob");
officeQueue.addClient("Charlie");
officeQueue.addClient("David");

// 3. Check the current line (who is waiting)
console.log("\n--- Current Waiting List ---");
console.log(officeQueue.getCurrentLine());

// 4. Announce the next person (removes them from the queue)
console.log("\n--- Announcing Next Client ---");
const nextPerson = officeQueue.getNextClient();
if (nextPerson) {
  console.log(`> Please call ${nextPerson.name} (Ticket #${nextPerson.ticketNumber}).`);
}

// 5. Add a new person while the line is being processed
console.log("\n--- New Arrival ---");
officeQueue.addClient("Eve");

// 6. Check the line again to see the updated order
console.log("\n--- Updated Waiting List ---");
console.log(officeQueue.getCurrentLine());

// 7. Call the next person
console.log("\n--- Announcing Next Client ---");
const nextPerson2 = officeQueue.getNextClient();
if (nextPerson2) {
  console.log(`> Please call ${nextPerson2.name} (Ticket #${nextPerson2.ticketNumber}).`);
}
```

### How it Works

1.  **State Management**: The `this.queue` array acts as the source of truth. Because it is stored inside the class instance (`officeQueue`), the data persists as long as the script is running.
2.  **FIFO (First In, First Out)**:
    *   We use `.push()` when adding a client to ensure they go to the very back of the line.
    *   We use `.shift()` when getting the next client, which removes the person at the very front and returns them. This automatically updates the list.
3.  **Ticket Numbers**:
    *   The `_generateTicketNumber` method ensures tickets are sequential (1, 2, 3...).
    *   You can also pass a custom ticket number if your office uses a specific numbering system (e.g., "A-105").
4.  **Safety**: The `getCurrentLine()` method returns a **copy** (`[...this.queue]`). This prevents external code from accidentally modifying the internal queue structure directly.

### Integration Tips

*   **Web Interface**: You can call `officeQueue.getNextClient()` from a button click event on your website. The function returns an object, so you can easily inject the `name` and `ticketNumber` into your HTML DOM to display it on a screen.
*   **Audio**: To "announce" via audio, simply check the return value:
    ```javascript
    const client = officeQueue.getNextClient();
    if (client) {
      const utterance = new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(`Please call ${client.name}, ticket number ${client.ticketNumber}`);
      window.speechSynthesis.speak(utterance);
    }
    ```
*   **Persistence**: If you refresh the page, the queue will reset. To keep the queue after a refresh, you would need to save the `this.queue` array to `localStorage` or a database after every `addClient` or `getNextClient` call.
